WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
Pull up a chair, this is Models and Agents, episode 64,

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.140
on May 30th, 2026.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:06.680
Let's see what shipped.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:09.920
Their signal and noise in AI every day.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.180
Let's get to the signal.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.740
Windows users can now steer codex agents directly

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.600
on their machines while stepping away.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.720
Open AI extended computer use capabilities

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:23.520
to windows for codex, letting the agent act on local desktops

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.620
and continue tasks from the mobile app.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:31.660
Step fun released a 198 billion MOE vision language model

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:36.060
optimized for coding agents, while Hermes agent added tool search

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.420
to reduce MCP context bloat.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.520
Builders should test the new Windows support this week

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.060
and evaluate the MIMO memory architecture

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.320
for long-term knowledge updates without retraining.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.660
Open AI added Windows support for codex computer use,

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.080
allowing the agent to perform actions directly

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.700
on Windows machines and continue tasks

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.340
started from the chat GPT mobile app.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:02.820
The feature enables users to start, review, and steer

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.660
work remotely while the agent keeps running locally.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.960
This expands codex beyond macOS and browser environments,

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.300
giving developers a path to run autonomous coding sessions

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.860
on standard Windows hardware.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.400
Early access means reliability is still limited

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.460
and users should expect iteration on edge cases

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.600
like window management and file permissions.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.400
Watch for expanded mobile to desktop handoff features

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.240
and tighter integration with local development tools

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.320
in the coming weeks.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.440
This development continues the steady maturation

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.140
of autonomous agents and tool use protocols

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:40.060
with reliability on long horizon task

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.960
remaining a key open question.

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:45.120
Step fun released step 3.7 flash,

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.260
a 198 billion MOE vision language model

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.080
with native vision support and 256,000 context.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.760
It includes an advisor mode aimed at coding agents

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.020
and search workflows.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.900
The model targets agent use cases

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.220
where vision and long context matter.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.420
Builders working on multimodal coding agents

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:07.720
should test it against current,

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.620
trend, and Gemini vision setups

00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.740
to see where the MOE routing helps.

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:15.180
Grif released Pantheon Reasoning 27b,

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:19.200
a dense twin 3.627b fine tune

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.060
that keeps thinking tags active

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.880
across multi-turn conversations.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:26.120
Training data combined Pantheon roleplay

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:29.180
with full reasoning traces from Opus 4.6,

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.020
WorldSim and text adventure sources.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.440
The model is positioned as a successor

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.240
to both the Pantheon series and earlier codex releases.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.780
Roleplay and agent developers can try the GGUFquants

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:44.900
to see whether the added reasoning traces

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:48.520
improve character consistency over non-reasoning baselines.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.000
Nvidia introduced X token,

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.320
a projection guided cross tokenizer,

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:55.080
knowledge distillation method,

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.960
that improves on gold.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:01.720
On lemma 3.21b, it raised average performance

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.160
by 3.82 points and lifted GSM 1000 accuracy

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.220
from 2.56 to 15.54.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:12.600
The technique addresses structural failures

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.680
in prior distillation approaches when tokenizers differ.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.140
Teams doing model compression or student teacher setups

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.260
should examine the method for small model reasoning gains.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:27.120
A detailed la-ma.cpp perplexity benchmark

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.540
compared multiple Q-Winth 3.6 to 27b quants

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.480
from unsloth, m-radar marker, and others

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:38.860
using mean KLD and top token match.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.460
Q-6K and Q-5KM variants stayed close to BF16

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:48.560
while Q-4K XL offered the best quality to size trade-off

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.500
for 16 gigabyte cards.

00:03:51.140 --> 00:03:54.960
Lower quants below Q-4 showed sharp KLD increases.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:59.560
Anyone running Q-Winth 3.6 to 27b locally

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:04.380
should review the tables before choosing between unsloth Q-4K XL

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.660
and radar marker IQ-4XS.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:12.860
A developer created a training script to convert Gemma-431b

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:17.580
dense into a native MOE model by adding a router and experts

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:20.880
while preserving the enable MOE block config.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.980
The experiment targets knowledge updates

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.820
and capability gains through the MOE structure.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.880
Early results are pending full runs on B300 hardware.

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:35.300
Researchers interested in post-training dense Gemma-4 models

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.160
should watch for public checkpoints and training code.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.980
Hermes agent added tool search to the MCP layer

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:45.120
using BM25 progressive schema disclosure.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:51.200
Andthropic evals showed accuracy gains from 49% to 74%

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.320
on Opus-4 by reducing context below.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.360
The update targets long horizon agents

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.780
that previously hit token limits when exposing many tools.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.160
Teams building MCP compatible agents

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:05.880
should test the new search mechanism

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.380
to see how it affects tool selection reliability.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:13.300
This update advances standardization of agent and tool protocols

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:15.960
directly addressing reliability questions

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.600
for long horizon agents.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:21.780
Genesis AI released Genesis World 1.0,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.980
a four component simulation platform covering physics,

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.640
rendering, compilation and tooling

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:29.900
for robotics foundation model evaluation.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:35.580
It achieved a 0.8996 Pearson correlation between simulation

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:39.880
and real world robot rollouts while cutting policy evaluation time

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:43.780
from over 200 hours to under 0.5 hours.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.040
NICS and quadrants components support scalable testing.

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:50.800
Robotics researchers and simulation teams

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.580
should evaluate the platform for faster iteration

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:54.880
on foundation models.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.120
Agent Trove provides 1.7 million

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:01.380
agentic interaction traces in shared GBT format,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:04.100
the largest open collection currently available.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:07.440
A Python tutorial shows how to stream the data,

00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:09.680
normalized turns, extract commands

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:14.160
and export successful trajectories into clean SFT data sets.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:17.100
The resource targets researchers building

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.940
or fine tuning agent models.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:22.160
Developers working on agent training pipelines

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.960
should pull the data set to augment existing SFT collections.

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:30.000
Folloc V2 runs a fully local voice assistant

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.480
on 16 gigabyte VRAM using Quen 3.5 to 9B for generation,

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:39.400
Quen 3.1.7B for ASR and TTS,

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:44.040
plus BGE embeddings for semantic search over obsidian vaults.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:46.360
It supports home assistant control,

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:49.800
agentic long-term memory, acoustic bargin

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:52.380
and custom wakewords without special models.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.340
The project includes a chat UI and scripts

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:56.640
for creating new voices.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:00.280
Linux and Windows users looking for private voice agents

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:03.320
should clone the repo and test the obsidian integration.

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:07.160
Shadow AI is a Windows-only voice first companion

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:10.540
that runs locally, supports any language made conversation,

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.800
includes web search via a local CRXNG instance

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:17.580
and builds persistent memory across sessions.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:21.220
It offers optional Google integration and skill learning

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.080
while keeping all data on device.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:27.720
The project is open source under agpl3.0

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:31.140
and uses a bring your own key model with Gemini.

00:07:31.980 --> 00:07:35.180
Windows developers wanting a always listening local assistant

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.140
should review the GitHub for setup

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.940
and planned local model additions.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.000
A community port of Flash attention too

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.360
for V100 GPUs delivered three to 24 time speedups

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.160
on forward and backward passes,

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.540
depending on sequence length and batch size,

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:54.100
with memory reductions up to 93%.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:57.360
Benchmarks covered causal and non-causal attention

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:00.020
across multiple head and dimension configurations.

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:03.560
V100 owners running older inference stacks

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:05.760
should test the AI bond implementation

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.200
to reclaim memory and reduce latency.

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:12.500
Test open AI codex computer use on a Windows machine

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.900
for local file and IDE tasks

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:18.300
to see how the mobile handoff performs in practice.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:24.760
Run the pantheon rezoning 27 BGGUF quant on a roleplay

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:28.760
or agent workflow and compare thinking trace consistency

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:32.340
against a standard twin 3.627B.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:36.500
Evaluate Hermes agent tool search on an MCP setup

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:39.460
with many tools to measure the reported accuracy lift

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:40.440
on Opus 4.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:47.520
Benchmark unslawed Q4KXL versus M radar marker IQ4XS

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:52.600
on Q-Went 3.6227B using your own prompts

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:55.440
to decide the right quant for 16 GB cards.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:59.420
Cloneful it to V2 and connected to your obsidian vault

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:02.400
to test semantic voice search over personal notes.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:05.060
More Windows and mobile extensions

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:07.440
for codex style computer use expected

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.260
as open AI iterates on the early release.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:14.780
Additional MOE conversion experiments on Gemma 431B

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:16.940
likely to appear on hugging face

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:19.160
following the initial proof of concept.

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:22.060
Expanded robotics simulation benchmarks

00:09:22.060 --> 00:09:24.340
using Genesis world 1.0

00:09:24.340 --> 00:09:27.740
as more teams adopt the platform for policy evaluation.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.140
Okay, let's pop the hood on memory models

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:33.420
versus RAG for continual updates.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.900
Everyone talks about giving LLM's new knowledge

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:40.080
as if retrieval or fine-tuning are the only two levers.

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:43.420
In practice, MIMO style memory models

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:45.620
insert a small dedicated network

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:47.940
that stores facts parametrically

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:49.780
while the main model stays frozen.

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.540
The memory model is trained on synthetic QA pairs

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:55.080
distilled from new documents

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:57.560
then updated via task vector merging

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.000
when fresh data arrives.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:01.680
This avoids the catastrophic forgetting

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.560
that occurs when you fine-tune the full LLM.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:06.880
Inside steps, the context window

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:09.020
and noise problems of RAG.

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.140
The trade-off is upfront compute.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.760
Roughly 180H200 GPU hours to train a 14B memory model

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:20.180
plus another 240 hours to generate reflections

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:23.400
plus an 11% to 19% accuracy drop

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:24.940
versus full retraining.

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:28.480
The approach shines when your corpus changes slowly

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:31.040
and you need synthesis across many documents

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:32.560
rather than exact look up.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:36.560
Use it when you already have a strong frozen reasoning model

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.700
and want to swap in updated knowledge

00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.460
without touching the base weights.

00:10:41.260 --> 00:10:43.800
Stick with RAG when you need source citations

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:45.720
or face rapidly changing data.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:50.020
Before we go, keep an eye on further memory model merging results

00:10:50.600 --> 00:10:52.420
and smaller scale MIMO variants

00:10:52.420 --> 00:10:55.920
as researchers optimize the reflection generation pipeline.

